\sbknone\linex0\sectdefaultcl {\footer \pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\tqc\tx4779\tqr\tx9558\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 
\par }}{\*\pnseclvl1\pnucrm\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxta .}}{\*\pnseclvl2\pnucltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxta .}}{\*\pnseclvl3\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxta .}}{\*\pnseclvl4\pnlcltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxta )}}
{\*\pnseclvl5\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl6\pnlcltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl7\pnlcrm\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl8
\pnlcltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl9\pnlcrm\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}\pard\plain \s17\qc \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\tx204\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 
\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b\f1\fs38 Fleet race boat rotations
\par }{\b\f1 Taken from the \'93}{\b\i\f1 Race Management Handbook}{\b\f1 \'94, }{\b\f1 {\field{\*\fldinst SYMBOL 227 \\f "Symbol" \\s 12}{\fldrslt\f3\fs24}}}{\b\f1  2001, US SAILING
\par Chapter 5; 5.2, 5.3
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s18\qj \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\tx589\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b\f1\fs30 \'93Follow the leader\'94 rotations
\par }\pard\plain \s19\qj \li136\ri0\sl-232\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx725\faauto\rin0\lin136\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 \'93Follow the leader\'94
 rotations can be used with any number of entries, odd or even. Their advan\-tages are that they are easy for crews to remember, and are easily adaptable when established in advance, if fewer crews than expected appear for the regatta.
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sl-232\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx725\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \s19\qj \li136\ri0\sl-232\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx725\faauto\rin0\lin136\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 The drawbacks to \'93follow-the-leader\'94
 rotations are that: (1) each crew to use a boat always fol\-lows the same crew which has previously used the boat, so it is easy for one crew to affect another\rquote 
s standing; and (2) they require a larger number of time-consuming landings and exchanges, ei\-ther on the exchange boat or ashore.
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sl-232\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx725\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \s19\qj \li136\ri0\sl-232\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx725\faauto\rin0\lin136\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 Each crew draws a boat number for the first ra
ce, and thereafter sails the next highest (or lowest) number each succeeding race (there is no magic to use of a higher-numbered or lower num\-bered method, only personal preference).
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sl-232\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx450\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs22 
